#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350} :activity/id #uuid "0a5bdaa9-fe87-516a-8c94-9bc96565f389" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/id #uuid "b412ece5-8f53-54cb-9936-346283210a0b" :topic/math-academy-id 2225} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139667 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-4 \\\\ 6x-y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/59/59f16d57e74fd94fdab464eca22cea1271b19a3b78114446e31343cfabd15a12.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6).$\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations. \n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & \\,=\\,-4 \\\\ (2)-(6) & =-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & \\,=\\,6 \\\\ 6(2)-6 & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." :tutorial/id #uuid "84e2025e-9a3e-50ad-9b6b-dcc75fb2b7c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "331a78d4-5c29-520f-a140-cfab1b9cf26b" :step/math-academy-id 196434 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138083 :question/id #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13129"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f80bc47-7b56-5357-b4e7-718f4ddfa41b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495142}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "bbbe689a-fd82-54c6-b18a-876c6124c4f7" :step/math-academy-id 196435 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138084 :question/id #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4154d81b-c94a-57f7-80fa-0daa341505e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495144}] :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "3a86bd4f-c706-59ab-986e-311feb0009df" :step/math-academy-id 196439}] :activity/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138586}]}
